// Code.
//
// Written against the sources (#178): `crates/wcl_wdoc/lib/code.wcl`,
// `src/highlight.rs`, `src/render/content_html.rs`,
// `src/render/include.rs`, `src/markdown/content.rs` and
// `src/pdf/content.rs`. Every listing, tag and command below was built and
// read back before it was written down — including the language-tag table,
// which was probed against the loaded syntax set rather than copied from
// the older reference page, and the file-backed listings, which were built
// against real files on disk.
page wdoc_code {
  title = "Code"

  chapter_header "Code" {
    kicker       = "wdoc · reference"
    reading_time = "16 min read"
    updated      = "2026-08-21"
    version      = "wcl 0.33.2-alpha"
  }

  p "A `code` block is a source listing: a language tag, some text, and an optional filename. It is the block a technical document uses most, and it is deliberately thin. It measures nothing, draws no chrome, and knows nothing about the page around it. It hands the renderer a listing and the language it is written in, and each output target draws that listing the way its own medium wants."

  p "The text comes from one of two places. Write it into the document with `source`, or name a file with `source_file` and let the build read it. The second is what keeps a manual honest about code that lives somewhere else."

  p "This chapter covers the block and its fields, reading a listing from a file, the language tags that resolve to a grammar, and what HTML, Markdown and PDF each make of one listing. Every example below was built and read back before it was written down."

  h2 "One listing, three outputs"

  p "Save this as `listing.wcl`. It is a whole document, with the import, a site, a page, and one `code` block:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "listing.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
# listing.wcl — one page, one listing.
import <wdoc.wcl>

site manual {
  title = "Manual"
}

page install {
  title = "Install"

  h1 "Install"

  p "Fetch the archive, unpack it, and put the binary on your path:"

  code bash {
    filename = "install.sh"
    source = <<'SH'
set -euo pipefail

curl -fsSL https://example.com/tool.tar.gz -o tool.tar.gz
tar -xzf tool.tar.gz
install -m 755 tool/bin/tool /usr/local/bin/tool
SH
  }
}
WCL
  }

  p "The example uses two heredocs. The outer one is the WCL you are reading. The inner one is the listing itself. Both are **raw** heredocs, `<<'TAG'` with the tag quoted, so nothing inside either is escaped or interpolated. That is the form to reach for every time. See [Values and primitives](lang_values) for the whole heredoc grammar."

  p "Build it three ways:"

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check listing.wcl
OK
$ wcl wdoc build listing.wcl --out _site
wrote 1 page
$ wcl wdoc build listing.wcl --out _md --type markdown
wrote 1 page
$ wcl wdoc build listing.wcl --out manual.pdf --type pdf
wrote 1 pdf
CONSOLE
  }

  p "One block produced three renderings. The Markdown one is the easiest to read back whole, so start there:"

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ cat _md/install.md
# Install

Fetch the archive, unpack it, and put the binary on your path:

`install.sh`

```bash
set -euo pipefail

curl -fsSL https://example.com/tool.tar.gz -o tool.tar.gz
tar -xzf tool.tar.gz
install -m 755 tool/bin/tool /usr/local/bin/tool
```
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The filename became a line of its own. The language tag became the fence's info string. Nothing else survived, because nothing else means anything in Markdown. [What each backend draws](#what-each-backend-draws) works through the other two."

  h2 "The fields"

  p "`Code` declares nine fields and no more. There is no width, no theme, and no highlight-these-lines list."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Field"     | "Type"        | "Written as"                     | "What it does" |
      | "`language`" | "`identifier`" | "the inline label, `code bash {`"  | "Picks the highlight grammar and labels the listing" |
      | "`source`"  | "`utf8?`"     | "`source = <<'SH' … SH`"          | "The listing text, verbatim" |
      | "`source_file`" | "`utf8?`" | "`source_file = \"install.sh\"`"   | "Read the listing from this file instead. See [Reading a listing from a file](#reading-a-listing-from-a-file)" |
      | "`anchor`"  | "`utf8?`"     | "`anchor = \"setup\"`"            | "Take only the marked region of `source_file`" |
      | "`lines`"   | "`utf8?`"     | "`lines = \"12-30\"`"             | "Take only that line range of `source_file`" |
      | "`dedent`"  | "`bool?`"     | "`dedent = true`"                 | "Strip the indentation the selected region shares" |
      | "`filename`" | "`utf8?`"    | "`filename = \"install.sh\"`"      | "Names the listing. See [Filename and caption](#filename-and-caption)" |
      | "`id`"      | "`identifier?`" | "`id = \"install\"`"             | "HTML only: the `id` attribute on the `<pre>`" |
      | "`class`"   | "`list<utf8>?`" | "`class = [\"wide\"]`"           | "HTML only: added beside `code-block` on the `<pre>`" |
  }

  p "`source` and `source_file` are both optional in the schema, and **exactly one of them must be set**. Neither leaves nothing to render; both would make the build choose a winner, and the wrong guess is a listing that silently disagrees with the file beside it. Either way the build stops and says so."

  p "`id` and `class` are HTML-shaped, and they behave that way: the Markdown and PDF backends read the same lowered listing and drop both. Reach for them when a stylesheet or a link needs to address one listing on a page, not when you want the listing to look different everywhere."

  h3 "Writing the source"

  p "`source` is an ordinary `utf8?` field, so any string expression fills it. When a listing is written into the document rather than read from a file, it is almost always a raw heredoc, for one reason: a listing is full of the characters a plain string treats as syntax."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
# Right: a raw heredoc. Backslashes and ${…} are literal text.
code python {
  source = <<'PY'
path = f"{root}\n{name}"
PY
}

# Wrong: an interpolating heredoc. `${root}` is evaluated as WCL.
code python {
  source = $<<PY
path = f"{root}"
PY
}
WCL
  }

  p "Nothing in `source` runs through the inline-pattern engine either. A listing containing `**stars**` or `[brackets](x)` keeps them exactly as typed. The patterns apply to prose, not to code. See [Text and formatting](wdoc_formatting)."

  h2 "Reading a listing from a file"

  p "A listing written into the document is a copy of code that lives somewhere else. Nothing keeps the two in step, and nothing notices when they drift apart. A manual with two hundred listings has two hundred places where the page can quietly stop being true."

  p "`source_file` names the file instead, and the build reads it:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
code rust {
  source_file = "../src/retry.rs"
}
WCL
  }

  p "The path is resolved against the directory of the document that names it — not the entry document, and not the working directory. A relative path may climb out of the doc tree, which is the ordinary case: the code a manual quotes usually lives beside the manual rather than under it. An absolute path is taken as given."

  h3 "Taking part of a file"

  p "A whole file is rarely the listing you want. Narrow it two ways."

  p "**`anchor`** takes the region between two marker comments in the quoted file:"

  code rust {
    filename = "src/retry.rs"
    source = <<'RS'
use std::time::Duration;

// ANCHOR: backoff
pub fn backoff(attempt: u32) -> Duration {
    Duration::from_secs(2u64.pow(attempt))
}
// ANCHOR_END: backoff
RS
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
code rust {
  source_file = "../src/retry.rs"
  anchor      = "backoff"
}
WCL
  }

  p "The match is on the text `ANCHOR: name`, anywhere on the line, so the marker sits in whatever comment syntax the quoted file uses. `// ANCHOR: x`, `# ANCHOR: x` and `<!-- ANCHOR: x -->` all work, and wdoc never needs to know the language to find them."

  p "**`lines`** takes a 1-based inclusive range. Write it four ways: `\"12-30\"`, `\"12-\"` to the end, `\"-30\"` from the start, or `\"12\"` for a single line."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
code rust {
  source_file = "../src/retry.rs"
  lines       = "3-7"
}
WCL
  }

  p "Prefer anchors. A line range is addressed by position, so it silently means something different the moment anyone inserts a line above it — which is the drift this feature exists to stop. An anchor moves with the code it marks. Setting both `anchor` and `lines` on one block fails the build rather than picking a precedence rule nobody would remember."

  callout "Markers never reach the page" {
    kind = :note
    body  = "An `ANCHOR:` comment is bookkeeping addressed to wdoc, so it is dropped from every listing — anchored, line-ranged, or whole-file. Anchors may nest, and an inner one's markers vanish from the outer region too. The listing above, which *shows* the markers, is written inline with `source` for exactly that reason."
  }

  h3 "Indentation"

  p "A region lifted out of a nested scope arrives with the indentation of where it used to live. Anchor a method inside an `impl` block and every line starts four columns in:"

  code rust {
    filename = "src/retry.rs"
    source = <<'RS'
impl Policy {
    // ANCHOR: inner
    pub fn should_retry(&self, attempt: u32) -> bool {
        attempt < self.max_attempts
    }
    // ANCHOR_END: inner
}
RS
  }

  p "`dedent` strips the indentation every non-blank line shares, and leaves the structure inside it alone:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
code rust {
  source_file = "../src/retry.rs"
  anchor      = "inner"
  dedent      = true
}
WCL
  }

  p "The four shared columns go; the four inside the method body stay:"

  code rust {
    source = <<'RS'
pub fn should_retry(&self, attempt: u32) -> bool {
    attempt < self.max_attempts
}
RS
  }

  h3 "What names the listing"

  p "`filename` defaults to `source_file` when the listing comes from disk, so the card header names the file that was actually read. Set `filename` explicitly to label it something else — the repository-relative path, say, rather than the path the document had to walk to reach it:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
code rust {
  source_file = "../../crates/retry/src/lib.rs"
  filename    = "crates/retry/src/lib.rs"
}
WCL
  }

  h3 "When the file is not there"

  p "A read that fails **fails the build**. A missing file, an anchor the file does not mark, a line range past its end: each stops the build with a message naming the file, and exits 3."

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl wdoc build listing.wcl --out _site
wcl::eval::user_error

  × error: code block cannot read `source_file` "../src/gone.rs"
  │ (/home/me/docs/../src/gone.rs): No such file or directory (os error 2)
   ╭─[/home/me/docs/listing.wcl:2:14]
 1 │ import <wdoc.wcl>
 2 │ page index { code rust { source_file = "../src/gone.rs" } }
   ·              ──────────────────────┬─────────────────────
   ·                                    ╰── error raised here
   ╰────

$ echo $?
3
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The diagnostic points at the block, names the path as you wrote it, and gives the path it resolved to — which is the one to read when a relative path went somewhere you did not expect."

  p "That is the whole point of naming a file rather than copying it. Rendering an empty card, or last build's text, would put back exactly the silent drift the field exists to catch. A listing that names a file is a listing the build is promising to keep true."

  callout "`wcl check` does not catch it" {
    kind = :warning
    body  = "`check` parses and validates against the schema; it never renders, and the file is read at render time. A `source_file` pointing at nothing passes `wcl check` and fails `wcl wdoc build`. Put the build in CI, not just the check."
  }

  callout "`wdoc serve` does not watch the file" {
    kind = :note
    body  = "The dev server's watcher tracks `.wcl` sources only, so editing the quoted code does not add to its pending-changes note. Rebuilds are manual anyway — press Enter — and a full rebuild re-reads every file-backed listing, so the change does land. It just is not what prompts you to ask for it."
  }

  h2 "The language tag"

  p "The tag is the block's inline label, so you write it before the brace and nowhere else:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
code rust {
  source = "fn main() {}"
}
WCL
  }

  p "It does two jobs at once, and they are worth keeping apart. It **picks the grammar** the highlighter uses. It is also **shown verbatim**: as the label in the HTML card's header bar, and as the fence info string in Markdown. The two can disagree, and that is not always a mistake: a tag no grammar answers to still labels the listing and still produces a fence a Markdown consumer may know how to colour."

  h3 "Which tags resolve"

  p "wdoc bundles 214 grammars in all: the syntect defaults, two-face's curated extras, and WCL's own. So `code wcl { … }` highlights the language this book is written in. A tag resolves in two steps:"

  list {
    style = :numbered
    li "Match the tag against every grammar's **file-extension** list, ignoring case. `rs` finds Rust. `md` finds Markdown. `yml` finds YAML."
    li "Otherwise match the grammar's **name**, ignoring case. `python` finds Python. `Rust` finds Rust. `dockerfile` finds Dockerfile."
  }

  p "No match means plain text: the listing renders, escaped and un-coloured. Here are the tags a technical document reaches for most, all checked against the loaded set:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Grammar"       | "Tags that reach it"   | "Grammar"     | "Tags that reach it" |
      | "WCL"           | "`wcl`"                | "JSON"        | "`json`" |
      | "Rust"          | "`rust`, `rs`"         | "YAML"        | "`yaml`, `yml`" |
      | "Python"        | "`python`, `py`"       | "TOML"        | "`toml`" |
      | "JavaScript"    | "`javascript`, `js`"   | "XML"         | "`xml`" |
      | "TypeScript"    | "`typescript`, `ts`"   | "INI"         | "`ini`" |
      | "TypeScriptReact" | "`tsx`"              | "Markdown"    | "`markdown`, `md`" |
      | "Go"            | "`go`"                 | "SQL"         | "`sql`" |
      | "C"             | "`c`"                  | "Diff"        | "`diff`" |
      | "C++"           | "`cpp`, `\"c++\"`"     | "HTML"        | "`html`" |
      | "C#"            | "`\"c#\"`, `cs`"       | "CSS"         | "`css`" |
      | "Java"          | "`java`"               | "SCSS"        | "`scss`" |
      | "Kotlin"        | "`kotlin`"             | "Dockerfile"  | "`dockerfile`" |
      | "Ruby"          | "`ruby`, `rb`"         | "Makefile"    | "`makefile`, `make`" |
      | "PHP"           | "`php`"                | "CMake"       | "`cmake`" |
      | "Swift"         | "`swift`"              | "Terraform"   | "`terraform`, `hcl`" |
      | "Haskell"       | "`haskell`, `hs`"      | "GraphQL"     | "`graphql`" |
      | "Scala"         | "`scala`"              | "Protocol Buffer" | "`proto`, `protobuf`" |
      | "Lua"           | "`lua`"                | "nginx"       | "`nginx`" |
      | "Elixir"        | "`elixir`"             | "LaTeX"       | "`tex`, `latex`" |
      | "Nix"           | "`nix`"                | "reStructuredText" | "`rst`" |
      | "Zig"           | "`zig`"                | "Bourne Again Shell (bash)" | "`bash`, `sh`, `zsh`" |
      | "Julia"         | "`julia`"              | "Fish"        | "`fish`" |
      | "Plain Text"    | "`txt`"                | "AWK"         | "`awk`" |
  }

  p "The list is not the limit. Anything with a Sublime grammar in the bundled set is reachable the same way: Clojure, Erlang, Dart, Perl, OCaml, F# (as `\"f#\"`), R, VimL, Typst, Svelte, Vue, GLSL, WGSL, jsonnet, x86 assembly and about a hundred and fifty more."

  h3 "Tags that do not resolve"

  p "Some obvious-looking tags find nothing, because neither an extension nor a grammar name spells them that way. Each of these renders as plain text:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Tag"      | "What happens"        | "Write instead" |
      | "`shell`"  | "plain text"          | "`sh`, `bash` or `zsh` for a script, `console` for a session" |
      | "`plaintext`" | "plain text"       | "`text` or `txt`" |
      | "`csharp`" | "plain text"          | "`\"c#\"` or `cs`" |
      | "`fsharp`" | "plain text"          | "`\"f#\"`" |
      | "`powershell`" | "plain text"      | "nothing. No PowerShell grammar ships" |
  }

  p "`text` is the useful one for output with no prompts in it. No grammar is named `text` and no file extension is `text`, so `code text { … }` falls straight through to plain text. `txt` reaches the real Plain Text grammar and renders identically."

  h3 "`console` for a terminal session"

  p "A transcript is not a script. It interleaves what you typed with what the command printed back, so a script grammar is the wrong tool: `bash` tokenises the output as source, and an apostrophe in a diagnostic such as `field 'replicas' is below @min(0)` opens a string literal that mis-colours the rest of the line."

  p "`console` is a session grammar, bundled alongside the WCL one because no shell-session grammar ships with the syntax set. It colours the prompt marker and the command you ran, and leaves every other line untouched:"

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check app.wcl --json
wcl::eval::schema_violation

  × field 'replicas': value -1 is below @min(0)

app.wcl: 1 schema violation
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The prompt, the program name and its options carry token classes. The four output lines carry none, so they render exactly as `text` would have. Tag a session `console`, a shell _script_ `bash`, and bare output with no `$` line `text`."

  callout "A tag with punctuation must be quoted" {
    kind = :note
    body  = "The tag is an `identifier`, and `c++`, `c#`, and `f#` are not valid bare identifiers. Quote them, `code \"c#\" { … }`, exactly as you would quote any other block label. The quotes are about the text, not the declared type. See [Documents, fields and blocks](lang_documents)."
  }

  callout "A missing tag is not caught" {
    kind = :warning
    body  = "The schema declares `language` as a required field, but nothing currently refuses a block with no label at all: `code { source = \"…\" }` passes `wcl check`, renders as plain text, and emits an empty language label and a bare `class=\"language-\"`. That is a gap, not a feature. Always write a tag. Write `text` when there is no language."
  }

  h2 "Filename and caption"

  p "`filename` is the only caption a listing has. There is no `caption` field on `code`. The filename **is** the caption, and each backend puts it where a caption belongs in that medium."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
code python {
  filename = "fib.py"
  source = <<'PY'
def fib(n):
    return n if n < 2 else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)
PY
}
WCL
  }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Target"   | "Where the filename goes" |
      | "HTML"     | "The card's header bar, as `<span class=\"code-name\">`, left of the language label" |
      | "Markdown" | "A backticked line immediately above the fence" |
      | "PDF"      | "A monospace caption paragraph immediately above the listing" |
  }

  p "Leave it out and each target drops that part: the HTML header bar keeps its window dots and language label, and Markdown and PDF start at the listing. A filename is worth writing whenever the reader is meant to save the listing somewhere. In a document that teaches, that is most of the time."

  p "A listing read from a file names itself. When `source_file` is set and `filename` is not, the path becomes the caption — see [What names the listing](#what-names-the-listing)."

  h2 "`code` is not a native block"

  p "Most of the heavyweight blocks in wdoc are **native**: a terminal, a tilemap, a tree, a table. wdoc intercepts a native block in Rust because its output is not expressible in WCL: measured widget layout, calendar arithmetic, an ANSI grid. See [Writing your own blocks](wdoc_extending) for what that means and what it costs."

  p "`code` is not one of them. It is an ordinary `ContentBlock` with an ordinary `lower`, and the whole of it is this:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "code.wcl (abridged)"
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("code")
type Code extends ContentBlock {
  @inline(0) language: identifier
  source:      utf8?
  source_file: utf8?
  anchor:      utf8?
  lines:       utf8?
  dedent:      bool?
  filename:    utf8?
  id:          identifier?
  class:       list<utf8>?
  lower = fn(c: Code) -> list<Content> [
    Content::Code {
      source:      c.source,
      source_file: c.source_file,
      anchor:      c.anchor,
      lines:       c.lines,
      dedent:      c.dedent,
      language:    c.language,
      filename: if c.filename == none { c.source_file } else { c.filename },
      id:          c.id,
      class:       c.class,
    },
  ]
}
WCL
  }

  p "`Content::Code` is a node of the semantic content IR: the closed, target-neutral union every backend matches exhaustively. The lowering carries the fields across and stops. It does not build a card. It does not pick a colour. It does not know which target is running. It does not read the file either — that happens once, later, to the node."

  p "The alternative is worth picturing, because it is the shape this block deliberately does not have. Build the card in the lowering and the header bar becomes HTML markup. A second target that wants a filename then has to reach past the lowering and re-read the block's own fields to get one. One payload and three readings of it is what keeps a field from meaning something on one target and nothing on the next. [Output targets](wdoc_outputs) covers the union and the backends in full."

  p "Two consequences follow, and both are practical:"

  list {
    li "**A listing means the same thing everywhere.** Add a filename and it appears in all three outputs, in the shape each one uses for a caption. There is no target where the field is quietly ignored."
    li "**Your own block can produce a listing.** A `@block` type extending `ContentBlock` may return a `Content::Code` from its `lower`, and it inherits all three renderings for free. Neither a generated-config block nor a `manifest` block that prints itself as JSON needs a line of Rust. See [Writing your own blocks](wdoc_extending)."
  }

  h2 "What each backend draws"

  p "One `Content::Code`, three chromes. Read the table down a column to see what one target does with a listing, and across a row to see where a field goes on each of them."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Aspect"        | "HTML, `wdoc build`"                     | "Markdown, `wdoc build --type markdown`" | "PDF, `wdoc build --type pdf`" |
      | "Chrome"        | "A `<figure class=\"code-card\">` with a header bar and three window dots" | "None" | "None" |
      | "Filename"      | "In the header bar"                      | "A backticked line above the fence" | "A monospace caption line" |
      | "Language tag"  | "In the header bar, upper-cased by CSS, and as `class=\"language-<tag>\"`" | "The fence info string" | "Not shown" |
      | "Highlighting"  | "`<span class=\"tok-…\">` runs, coloured by the theme's CSS" | "None. The consumer colours the fence" | "Coloured runs drawn into the page" |
      | "Line numbers"  | "A CSS counter gutter, always on"        | "None"                            | "None" |
      | "`id` / `class`" | "On the `<pre>`"                        | "Dropped"                         | "Dropped" |
  }

  h3 "The HTML code-card"

  p "The book card is a fixed shape. Build the `listing.wcl` from the top of this chapter and read `_site/install.html`, and the listing is exactly this (token spans elided):"

  code html {
    source = <<'HTML'
<figure class="code-card">
  <div class="code-filename">
    <span class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span>
    <span class="code-name">install.sh</span>
    <span class="code-lang">bash</span>
  </div>
  <pre class="code-block"><code class="language-bash"><span class="code-line">…</span>…</code></pre>
</figure>
HTML
  }

  p "Three things in there are worth knowing about. The `<span class=\"code-lang\">` holds the tag exactly as you wrote it, `bash` and not `BASH`. The upper-casing is a CSS rule on the class, so a theme may drop it. Every source line is wrapped in its own `<span class=\"code-line\">`, and the gutter numbers are a CSS counter on that wrapper rather than text in the markup. That is why copying a listing out of the page copies the code and not the numbers. And the gutter is not optional: HTML listings always carry line numbers."

  callout "Per-line highlighting restarts the grammar" {
    kind = :warning
    body  = "Wrapping each line separately is what makes the gutter work, and it has a real cost: the HTML highlighter starts each line from scratch. A construct that spans lines loses its scope after the first one. Give it a Rust block comment and line 1 is comment-coloured, while `spanning lines */` on line 2 comes back as ordinary source with `*` and `/` read as operators. Short listings, the kind a document carries, are unaffected. The PDF backend has no gutter and carries grammar state across lines, so the same listing prints correctly there. When a listing needs a long block comment, prefer line comments."
  }

  h3 "The Markdown fence"

  p "The one piece of real work Markdown does is the fence itself. The backend widens it past the longest backtick run in the source, so a listing that contains a fenced block still nests correctly."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
code markdown {
  filename = "readme.md"
  source = <<'MD'
Fence inside:

```rust
fn main() {}
```
MD
}
WCL
  }

  p "The three-backtick run inside forces a four-backtick fence around it:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
`readme.md`

````markdown
Fence inside:

```rust
fn main() {}
```
````
TEXT
  }

  p "Highlighting is not wdoc's job here. The tag rides out on the fence, and whatever renders the Markdown, a repository host or a static site, colours it with its own grammars. That is also why a tag no syntect grammar answers to is still worth writing: `code shell { … }` is plain text in the built site and a highlighted shell block on a repository host."

  h3 "The PDF listing"

  p "The PDF backend has no CSS to colour a `tok-` class with, so it does the colouring itself. It highlights the source into runs of text plus an RGB triple, against a light theme chosen to read on a white page, and draws those runs into the page. A filename becomes a monospace caption paragraph above the listing. There is no card, no gutter, and no way to restyle the token colours from the document. A PDF listing looks the same whichever theme the site selects. [Output targets](wdoc_outputs) covers the rest of the PDF backend."

  h2 "Colouring the tokens"

  p "In HTML, every token is a `<span>` carrying its syntect scope as a run of classes with a `tok-` prefix, outermost scope first. A Rust `fn` keyword comes out as:"

  code html {
    source = <<'HTML'
<span class="tok-storage tok-type tok-function tok-rust">fn</span>
HTML
  }

  p "The bundled rules target the short, stable ends of those scopes: `.tok-keyword`, `.tok-string`, `.tok-comment`, `.tok-constant`, `.tok-entity.tok-name.tok-function` and a few more. Each one resolves a palette variable with a literal fallback, so a document with no theme still gets a readable light palette."

  p "So the ordinary way to recolour code is not to write CSS at all. A theme's palette carries seven syntax roles. Set them and every listing on the site follows:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
theme midnight {
  palette dark {
    syn_kw      = "#c678dd"
    syn_str     = "#98c379"
    syn_num     = "#d19a66"
    syn_fn      = "#61afef"
    syn_type    = "#e5c07b"
    syn_comment = "#7f848e"
    syn_punct   = "#abb2bf"
  }
}

site manual {
  title = "Manual"
  theme = :midnight
}
WCL
  }

  p "Some scopes have no bundled rule, and the card chrome has none either. For anything the roles do not reach, declare a `class` block against the `tok-` selector or against `code-card`, `code-filename`, `code-name`, `code-lang` and `code-block`. [Styling rules](wdoc_css) covers palettes, roles and `class` blocks in full."

  h2 "Where to go next"

  list {
    li "[Text and formatting](wdoc_formatting). Prose, headings, and the backtick inline pattern, which is a different thing from this block."
    li "[Demo blocks](wdoc_demo). Show a snippet and its rendered result side by side. A demo is a `code` block plus a live rendering."
    li "[Output targets](wdoc_outputs). The content IR, the three backends, and what each one can and cannot express."
    li "[Themes](wdoc_styling). The seven `syn_*` roles a palette declares for code."
    li "[Styling rules](wdoc_css). The `class` block a `tok-` scope or a card element is restyled through."
    li "[Visibility](wdoc_visibility). `@only` and `@except`, for the listing that should reach one target and not another."
    li "[Writing your own blocks](wdoc_extending). `lower`, `@native`, and how a block of yours can produce a listing."
    li "[Values and primitives](lang_values). Heredocs, raw and interpolating, in full."
  }
}
